[[:db/retract 17592186157599 :question/answer-fields 17592186153838] {:db/id 17592186157355 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e41be435-8d57-5723-adfd-8b8482c6dcfd" "question-b36d14c1-4f6a-5e94-9ad9-d536b9f80b59" "question-8d4c868b-7564-5dcc-a2d7-26fd40beb90e" "question-da6b0f51-7da3-5f92-8b12-f21abc3a1502" "question-1efe5ada-0d49-54bc-8531-cf777586f9d6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157172 :question/problem "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an estimate for the population mean.\n\n$$\n6, \\quad 13, \\quad 7, \\quad 5, \\quad 14 , \\quad 11, \\quad 7\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with the population mean $\\mu.$ \nThen, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \n= \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i.\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the mean value of $X_1,X_2,\\ldots, X_n,$ while $\\bar{x}$ is the mean of a specific sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$\n\n- the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an estimator of the population mean $\\mu,$ and\n\n- the value $\\hat{\\mu} = \\bar{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean $\\mu.$\n\nTherefore, our estimate for the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\mu } & =\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(6+13+7+5+14+11+7) \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{7} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157392 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-df0700fa-3993-5f7a-8bb1-bfc5c621da09" "question-d56155bc-2d9b-51b2-8b0f-a98518988434" "question-8bcbbb4b-5f73-5c63-9997-c0acc2c3bd21" "question-e19c3513-ab57-5bed-8729-ff5ec68ea4f7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186156993 :question/problem "A sample $X_1, X_2$ of two metallic rings is drawn from a population. The population has rings of two different diameters, $16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}$ and $\\textrm{18}\\,\\textrm{mm},$ distributed in the ratio $5:1.$ Calculate $\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}].$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu.$ Then, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \n= \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the rings in the population have diameters $16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}$ and $\\textrm{18}\\,\\textrm{mm},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $5:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population mean, we can consider a population consisting of $5+1 = 6$ rings only with the following diameters:\n\n$$\n16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 18\\,\\textrm{mm}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(5\\cdot 16.5+18) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(82.5+18) \\\\  & =\\frac{100.5}{6} \\\\  & =16.75\\,\\text{mm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu = 16.75\\,\\textrm{mm}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-e41be435-8d57-5723-adfd-8b8482c6dcfd" :question/id #uuid "e41be435-8d57-5723-adfd-8b8482c6dcfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-149731" :question/problem "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an estimate for the population mean.\n\n$$\n2,\\,-7,\\,15,\\,-3\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with the population mean $\\mu .$ \nThen, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the mean value of ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n},$ while $\\overset{¯}{x}$ is the mean of a specific sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$\n\n- the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an estimator of the population mean $\\mu ,$ and\n\n- the value $\\overset{^}{\\mu }=\\overset{¯}{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean $\\mu .$\n\nTherefore, our estimate for the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\mu } & =\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2-7+15-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4} \\\\  & =1.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8e16ce8b-c0fd-5bcf-aeff-4ee1bde189c1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8e16ce8b-c0fd-5bcf-aeff-4ee1bde189c1" :answer-field/id #uuid "8e16ce8b-c0fd-5bcf-aeff-4ee1bde189c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-24294c8f-833d-5c05-bbe8-7de4894ddace" :answer-field/choices ["answer-24294c8f-833d-5c05-bbe8-7de4894ddace" "answer-143d8ae2-eeb0-5785-9404-9aa0bd8db8b0" "answer-0a8f0bd0-23b0-55c2-ac0d-7b2cdb3481d1" "answer-aeae6d43-d40a-56db-bcb9-37288bd71888" "answer-1dfb0748-171c-5468-bfd1-1bcd7e123168"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24294c8f-833d-5c05-bbe8-7de4894ddace" :answer/id #uuid "24294c8f-833d-5c05-bbe8-7de4894ddace" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.75" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-143d8ae2-eeb0-5785-9404-9aa0bd8db8b0" :answer/id #uuid "143d8ae2-eeb0-5785-9404-9aa0bd8db8b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a8f0bd0-23b0-55c2-ac0d-7b2cdb3481d1" :answer/id #uuid "0a8f0bd0-23b0-55c2-ac0d-7b2cdb3481d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aeae6d43-d40a-56db-bcb9-37288bd71888" :answer/id #uuid "aeae6d43-d40a-56db-bcb9-37288bd71888" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1dfb0748-171c-5468-bfd1-1bcd7e123168" :answer/id #uuid "1dfb0748-171c-5468-bfd1-1bcd7e123168" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b36d14c1-4f6a-5e94-9ad9-d536b9f80b59" :question/id #uuid "b36d14c1-4f6a-5e94-9ad9-d536b9f80b59" :question/math-academy-id "q-139801" :question/problem "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an estimate for the population mean.\n\n$$\n1,\\,3,\\,9,\\,27,\\,81\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with the population mean $\\mu .$ \nThen, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the mean value of ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n},$ while $\\overset{¯}{x}$ is the mean of a specific sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$\n\n- the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an estimator of the population mean $\\mu ,$ and\n\n- the value $\\overset{^}{\\mu }=\\overset{¯}{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean $\\mu .$\n\nTherefore, our estimate for the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\mu } & =\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1+3+9+27+81) \\\\  & =\\frac{121}{5} \\\\  & =24.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b9109f22-06e5-578e-9cf9-5d992f213033"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b9109f22-06e5-578e-9cf9-5d992f213033" :answer-field/id #uuid "b9109f22-06e5-578e-9cf9-5d992f213033" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-153032cc-4283-545e-a6a0-cefb36c3114d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a380e234-66a5-5f40-b2a6