 population mean $\\mu .$\n\nTherefore, our estimate for the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\mu } & =\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(-8+5-14-20+16) \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{5} \\\\  & =-4.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-82968c5a-55cf-5036-a4f7-86e133d5bca1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-82968c5a-55cf-5036-a4f7-86e133d5bca1" :answer-field/id #uuid "82968c5a-55cf-5036-a4f7-86e133d5bca1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c5f2e157-ae58-57fd-a20d-e97626efe5ae" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0bad6d10-02bb-565e-9e3d-fc4d56f3515f" "answer-c5f2e157-ae58-57fd-a20d-e97626efe5ae" "answer-55e0499b-82a7-5555-ad32-f3b98d2d802e" "answer-f4a893b2-8685-577c-97ad-382d5e7f4531" "answer-19bf6f9a-c70f-54f2-bdbc-d7de758d6b9d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0bad6d10-02bb-565e-9e3d-fc4d56f3515f" :answer/id #uuid "0bad6d10-02bb-565e-9e3d-fc4d56f3515f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5f2e157-ae58-57fd-a20d-e97626efe5ae" :answer/id #uuid "c5f2e157-ae58-57fd-a20d-e97626efe5ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55e0499b-82a7-5555-ad32-f3b98d2d802e" :answer/id #uuid "55e0499b-82a7-5555-ad32-f3b98d2d802e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4a893b2-8685-577c-97ad-382d5e7f4531" :answer/id #uuid "f4a893b2-8685-577c-97ad-382d5e7f4531" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19bf6f9a-c70f-54f2-bdbc-d7de758d6b9d" :answer/id #uuid "19bf6f9a-c70f-54f2-bdbc-d7de758d6b9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-df0700fa-3993-5f7a-8bb1-bfc5c621da09" :question/id #uuid "df0700fa-3993-5f7a-8bb1-bfc5c621da09" :question/math-academy-id "q-149735" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ is a sample of size $n=15$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =-3$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1.44,$ what is $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu .$ Then, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu .\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the population mean $\\mu =-3,$ we have that $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cc516767-dda9-5091-abf1-97e902a2d79d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cc516767-dda9-5091-abf1-97e902a2d79d" :answer-field/id #uuid "cc516767-dda9-5091-abf1-97e902a2d79d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0f7bb12e-52c3-515c-8e46-fe08f075821a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bbf3defb-1569-5d81-a4fb-03770de6852d" "answer-0f7bb12e-52c3-515c-8e46-fe08f075821a" "answer-c2c76e11-4bf2-56c4-8221-5323bbb41e54" "answer-a94a9c5a-c845-581f-97a3-933376992ee7" "answer-83774d5d-3c08-5728-8476-2ae252a2ce57"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bbf3defb-1569-5d81-a4fb-03770de6852d" :answer/id #uuid "bbf3defb-1569-5d81-a4fb-03770de6852d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f7bb12e-52c3-515c-8e46-fe08f075821a" :answer/id #uuid "0f7bb12e-52c3-515c-8e46-fe08f075821a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2c76e11-4bf2-56c4-8221-5323bbb41e54" :answer/id #uuid "c2c76e11-4bf2-56c4-8221-5323bbb41e54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a94a9c5a-c845-581f-97a3-933376992ee7" :answer/id #uuid "a94a9c5a-c845-581f-97a3-933376992ee7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.44" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83774d5d-3c08-5728-8476-2ae252a2ce57" :answer/id #uuid "83774d5d-3c08-5728-8476-2ae252a2ce57" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d56155bc-2d9b-51b2-8b0f-a98518988434" :question/id #uuid "d56155bc-2d9b-51b2-8b0f-a98518988434" :question/math-academy-id "q-149733" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{20}$ is a sample of size $n=20$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =5$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.25,$ what is $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu .$ Then, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu .\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the population mean $\\mu =5,$ we have that $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fef1ad7e-8fc1-516b-bbb2-e5a760cd3158"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fef1ad7e-8fc1-516b-bbb2-e5a760cd3158" :answer-field/id #uuid "fef1ad7e-8fc1-516b-bbb2-e5a760cd3158" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c4720674-f78d-5a69-bec0-aba9f4f1238d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2c910f24-2d37-50fc-a152-3f25106a0c27" "answer-ac072746-ec16-5f8b-9610-31000e148c4c" "answer-a261d45f-0662-574d-bcfa-7d32b970f5cb" "answer-d41dc8fb-9c7b-5f9c-b53c-219bdc9f39ac" "answer-c4720674-f78d-5a69-bec0-aba9f4f1238d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c910f24-2d37-50fc-a152-3f25106a0c27" :answer/id #uuid "2c910f24-2d37-50fc-a152-3f25106a0c27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac072746-ec16-5f8b-9610-31000e148c4c" :answer/id #uuid "ac072746-ec16-5f8b-9610-31000e148c4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a261d45f-0662-574d-bcfa-7d32b970f5cb" :answer/id #uuid "a261d45f-0662-574d-bcfa-7d32b970f5cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d41dc8fb-9c7b-5f9c-b53c-219bdc9f39ac" :answer/id #uuid "d41dc8fb-9c7b-5f9c-b53c-219bdc9f39ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c4720674-f78d-5a69-bec0-aba9f4f1238d" :answer/id #uuid "c4720674-f78d-5a69-bec0-aba9f4f1238d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157599 :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2}$ of two nails is drawn from a population. The population has nails of two different lengths, $6\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{12}\\,\\text{cm},$ distributed in the ratio $3:1.$ Calculate $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}].$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu .$ Then, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu .\n$$\n\nWe're told that the nails in the population have lengths $6\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{12}\\,\\text{cm},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $3:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population mean, we can consider a population consisting of $3+1=4$ nails only with the following lengths:\n\n$$\n6\\,\\text{cm},\\,6\\,\\text{cm},\\,6\\,\\text{cm},\\,12\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3\\cdot 6+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(18+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{4} \\\\  & =7.5\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu =7.5\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5decded0-8969-541f-936f-5b210c73460d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5decded0-8969-541f-936f-5b210c73460d" :answer-field/id #uuid "5decded0-8969-541f-936f-5b210c73460d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-607fa761-c4b1-5186-83ab-55c0ba40778f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-607fa761-c4b1-5186-83ab-55c0ba40778f" "answer-4c983c9b-b809-585e-97ec-8977702a501c" "answer-2936c8de-b762-59f1-bba9-735fbc48a942" "answer-f00c7066-a8f5-5648-b370-55d63c9722ff" "answer-46d59859-24c5-5ddd-ac89-50051b7c27c6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-607fa761-c4b1-5186-83ab-55c0ba40778f" :answer/id #uuid "607fa761-c4b1-5186-83ab-55c0ba40778f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7.5\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c983c9b-b809-585e-97ec-8977702a501c" :answer/id #uuid "4c983c9b-b809-585e-97ec-8977702a501c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2936c8de-b762-59f1-bba9-735fbc48a942" :answer/id #uuid "2936c8de-b762-59f1-bba9-735fbc48a942" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8.25\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f00c7066-a8f5-5648-b370-55d63c9722ff" :answer/id #uuid "f00c7066-a8f5-5648-b370-55d63c9722ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8.5\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46d59859-24c5-5ddd-ac89-50051b7c27c6" :answer/id #uuid "46d59859-24c5-5ddd-ac89-50051b7c27c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8bcbbb4b-5f73-5c63-9997-c0acc2c3bd21" :question/id #uuid "8bcbbb4b-5f73-5c63-9997-c0acc2c3bd21" :question/math-academy-id "q-149734" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{70}$ is a sample of size $n=70$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =21$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=2,$ what is $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu .$ Then, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu .\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the population mean $\\mu =21,$ we have that $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=21.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-233863d7-ca4d-5090-aaeb-64f4e3730703"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-233863d7-ca4d-5090-aaeb-64f4e3730703" :answer-field/id #uuid "233863d7-ca4d-5090-aaeb-64f4e3730703" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9ed033a9-aeec-5491-9667-64e2697fc38c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bd6c71ec-77d3-5ba2-982d-2e2b41d01351" "answer-2ffef054-7fa7-58ea-9f8b-1cee6760ef0e" "answer-9ed033a9-aeec-5491-9667-64e2697fc38c" "answer-22806aa8-6678-5ff2-b5f1-43190f255865" "answer-a3a8936e-3a1b-5014-bbac-08d4902d7240"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd6c71ec-77d3-5ba2-982d-2e2b41d01351" :answer/id #uuid "bd6c71ec-77d3-5ba2-982d-2e2b41d01351" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ffef054-7fa7-58ea-9f8b-1cee6760ef0e" :answer/id #uuid "2ffef054-7fa7-58ea-9f8b-1cee6760ef0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ed033a9-aeec-5491-9667-64e2697fc38c" :answer/id #uuid "9ed033a9-aeec-5491-9667-64e2697fc38c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "21" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22806aa8-6678-5ff2-b5f1-43190f255865" :answer/id #uuid "22806aa8-6678-5ff2-b5f1-43190f255865" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "42" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3a8936e-3a1b-5014-bbac-08d4902d7240" :answer/id #uuid "a3a8936e-3a1b-5014-bbac-08d4902d7240" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "50" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e19c3513-ab57-5bed-8729-ff5ec68ea4f7" :question/id #uuid "e19c3513-ab57-5bed-8729-ff5ec68ea4f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-143253" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{45}$ is a sample of size $n=45$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =18$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=5,$ what is $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu .$ Then, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu .\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the population mean $\\mu =18,$ we have that $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=18.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2fd4ab83-1c57-57d4-b8c0-f5518f024ab9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2fd4ab83-1c57-57d4-b8c0-f5518f024ab9" :answer-field/id #uuid "2fd4ab83-1c57-57d4-b8c0-f5518f024ab9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7585894d-c7d4-5588-8e56-a8b0cbce5dec" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7585894d-c7d4-5588-8e56-a8b0cbce5dec" "answer-20801b48-3d06-5548-aaa9-748a5518e30f" "answer-dcb258d2-45fc-5505-87b1-ac1f22709c3d" "answer-d3e143f3-008a-5d50-becf-5741b1537f3b" "answer-99b35b45-8fb4-58a6-ba8e-c657276c3c40"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7585894d-c7d4-5588-8e56-a8b0cbce5dec" :answer/id #uuid "7585894d-c7d4-5588-8e56-a8b0cbce5dec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20801b48-3d06-5548-aaa9-748a5518e30f" :answer/id #uuid "20801b48-3d06-5548-aaa9-748a5518e30f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dcb258d2-45fc-5505-87b1-ac1f22709c3d" :answer/id #uuid "dcb258d2-45fc-5505-87b1-ac1f22709c3d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{18}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3e143f3-008a-5d50-becf-5741b1537f3b" :answer/id #uuid "d3e143f3-008a-5d50-becf-5741b1537f3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{18}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99b35b45-8fb4-58a6-ba8e-c657276c3c40" :answer/id #uuid "99b35b45-8fb4-58a6-ba8e-c657276c3c40" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158531 :tutorial/content "In statistics, we often want to use sample data to deduce (or **infer**) the values of various **population parameters**. The two most common population parameters that we wish to find are\n\n- the **population mean** $\\mu,$ and\n\n- the **population variance** $\\sigma^2.$\n\nThe population mean is the mean of the *entire* population. Likewise, the population variance is the variance of the *entire* population. We should consider these values as *fixed* unknowns. \n\nSuppose, for example, we want to find the mean number of pages per book at a particular library. In other words, we wish to find the population mean $\\mu.$ However, it is impractical to count the pages in every book. So, instead, we might approximate $\\mu$ using the following sampling procedure:\n\n- Randomly choose, say, $5$ books off the shelf.\n\n- Count the number of pages in each book.\n\n- Compute the mean of the $5$ observations.\n\nIn this case, the **sample size** $n=5.$\n\nSuppose we follow this procedure and get the following **observations**:\n\n$$\n201, \\qquad 506, \\qquad 58, \\qquad 104, \\qquad 99\n$$\n\nIt's convenient to denote the individual observations as $x_i.$ So, in this case, we have \n\n$$\nx_1 = 201,\\qquad x_2 = 506,\\qquad \\ldots,\\qquad x_5 = 99.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the mean $\\overline{x}$ of our sample as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(201+506+58+104+99) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 968 \\\\  & =193.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n The value $\\bar{x} = 193.6$ is called an **estimate** of the population mean $\\mu.$ We sometimes write $\\hat{\\mu} = 193.6$ to denote that the value $193.6$ is an estimate of $\\mu.$\n\nIt's important to realize that our estimate $\\bar{x}$ depends entirely on this particular sample. We'd get a different estimate for $\\mu$ if we took a different sample." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158560 :tutorial/content "It is helpful to think of the observations of a particular sample\n\n$$\nx_1,\\quad x_2,\\quad \\ldots,\\quad x_n\n$$\n\nas a particular instance of data generated by a sequence of random variables \n\n$$\nX_1,\\quad X_2,\\quad \\ldots,\\quad X_n.\n$$\n\nLet's make sure we're clear about the notation:\n\n- $X_i$ is a *random variable* that denotes the $i$th observation in a random sample. Its value changes when we conduct a new sample.\n\n- $x_i$ is the value of the $i$th observation in a particular sample.\n\nWe typically assume that our sequence of random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ has the following properties:\n\n- They are **independent and identically distributed** (or **I.I.D.**), meaning they are independent and have the same probability distribution. Moreover, they have the same distribution as the population.\n\n- Their mean is equal to the population mean:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}(X_i) = \\mu, \\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq n\n$$\n\n- Their variance is equal to the population variance:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}(X_i) = \\sigma^2, \\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq n\n$$\n\nWe often refer to a sequence of random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ with these properties as a **random sample of size** $\\boldsymbol{n}.$\n\nIn general, if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n,$ then the **sample mean** is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \n= \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i .\n$$\n\nWe say that the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an **estimator** of the population mean $\\mu.$\n\nSince $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ are random variables, so is $\\overline{X}.$ The probability distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is called the **sampling distribution** of $\\overline{X}.$ The sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is an important topic we'll discuss in detail in a future lesson." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158561 :tutorial/content "One important feature of $\\overline{X}$ is that it's an **unbiased estimator** of $\\mu.$ This means that the expected value of $\\overline{X}$ equals $\\mu,$ that is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can easily show that $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu$ using the properties of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\text{E}[\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot \\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot \\text{E}[{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot (\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{E}[{X}_{2}]+⋯+\\text{E}[{X}_{n}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot (\\mu +\\mu +⋯+\\mu ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot n\\mu  \\\\  & =\\mu \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fact that $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu$ is a desirable property. We'll meet so-called **biased estimators** in future lessons. \n\nFinally, any estimate $\\bar{x}$ of the population mean $\\mu$ found by computing the mean of a sample is called an **unbiased estimate** of $\\mu.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157528 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
